<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="very long chain fatty acid biosynthesis I" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5080" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Common Viridiplantae Pathways (CVP) generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most Viridiplantae species including algae. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; A significant amount of the fatty acids synthesized in animals by the cytosolic enzyme complex fatty acid synthase (FAS) or taken up by the diet are further elongated into |FRAME: Very-long-chain-fatty-acids &quot;very long chain fatty acids&quot;| (VLCFAs), which are at least 22 carbons in length. VLCFAs are also important in plants, where they are the main constituents of hydrophobic polymers (such as cuticular waxes, seed oils and membrane sphingolipids) that prevent dessication at the leaf surface and provide stability to pollen grains |CITS: [15277688]|. VLCFAs are synthesized in the endoplasmic reticulum by sequential addition of C2 moieties from |FRAME: MALONYL-COA| to preformed C18 acyl groups derived from the &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; fatty acid biosynthesis pathway. Each elongation cycle is composed of 4 reactions, similar to &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; fatty acid biosynthesis. The cycle includes the condensation of |FRAME: Long-Chain-Acyl-CoAs| and |FRAME: MALONYL-COA| to form |FRAME: 3-KETOACYL-COA|, reduction to |FRAME: 3-HYDROXYACYL-COA|, dehydration to |FRAME: TRANS-D2-ENOYL-COA|, and a final reduction to yield a chain-elongated fatty acyl-CoA |CITS:[16564093]|. Unlike in &lt;i&gt;de novo&lt;/i&gt; fatty acid biosynthesis, |FRAME: MALONYL-COA| rather than |FRAME: MALONYL-ACP| is used in the condensing reaction. The elongation cycle is catalyzed by a membrane-bound enzyme complex, the elongase |CITS: [10907783]|, which is composed of four enzymes, |FRAME: AT4G34520-MONOMER| or condensing enzyme |CITS: [11341960]|, 3-ketoacyl-CoA reductase, |FRAME: AT5G10480-MONOMER| and |FRAME: AT3G55360-MONOMER| |CITS: [15829606]|. Substrate and tissue specificity of VLCFAs elongation is determined by the condensing enzyme, which catalyzes the rate-limiting reaction. Both saturated and unsaturated fatty acids are elongated via this pathway. While both NADPH and NADH were shown to be an effective reducing power for the two reduction steps, in many cases NADPH is the &apos;preferred&apos; reductant (reviewed in |CITS: [8190743]| and |CITS: [16564093]|). For a pathway that specifies the intermediates involved, please see |FRAME: PWY-7036|.</Description>
